{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-84822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/6fcaa861fe507057f42c2194931bbc4a76d42891265ab40465832338cd8b5f4f.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,-1),$ and passes through the point $(0,-3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-4,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+4=2pt,\\,y+1=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(0,-3),$ we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+4=2(-2)t,\\,y+1=(-2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+4=-4t,\\,y+1=-2{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-84822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+4=-4t,\\,y+1=-2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+4=-4t,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+4=-t,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-4=-4t,\\,y-1=-2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-4=-t,\\,y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+4=-4t,\\,y+1=-2{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/4ac7f5cd3405e3839b829edc19c440c973521a0263f1aaa0a2f8245697779561.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,4),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\\,y-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+1=2t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+1=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and which passes through the point $(7,5)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=2pt,\\,y-3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,5),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=5$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{2}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=2(2)t,\\,y-3=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=4t,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+3=2t,\\,\\,y+3={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+3=4t,\\,\\,y+3=2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=2t,\\,\\,y-3={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=4{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y-3=2{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/0c37bb9e010bdb19de2afc8707b6cf1cef69638c9f4721c407c1593f3a387b87.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-2,-3),$ and passes through the point $(-12,-13).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=2pt,\\,y+3=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-12,-13),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=-13$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10=2pt \\\\ -10=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =2pt \\\\ -5 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{5}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =p{(-\\frac{5}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -10 & =p\\cdot \\frac{25}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -10 & =\\frac{25}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=2(-\\frac{5}{2})t,\\,y+3=(-\\frac{5}{2}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=-5t,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-106452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2=-10t,\\,\\,y+3=-5{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+2=-5t,\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+2=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+2=5t,\\,\\,y+3=-2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+2=5t,\\,\\,y+3=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+2=-5t,\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/bb83d1803b8b81992b8ed3f93019088beaba6db0fccf7ab707f03c55ed308678.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-1,2),$ and passes through the point $(-5,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=2pt,\\,y-k=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+1=2pt,\\,y-2=p{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,4),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=4$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4=2pt \\\\ 2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =2pt \\\\ -2 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(-\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+1=2(2)t,\\,y-2=(2){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+1=4t,\\,y-2=2{t}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84824" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-84824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+1=2t,\\,y-2=4{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=2t,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+1=4t,\\,y-2=2{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=2t,\\,y+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=4t,\\,y+2=2{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84824" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+1=4t,\\,y-2=2{t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/c6496d117c1b769474c353b0bfeef6836d21f080fb108752d55b8bb090dc0243.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-5,-3),$ and passes through the point $(7,9).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-5,-3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(7,9),$ we can substitute $x=7$ and $y=9$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+3=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+3=6t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-106460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+3=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=6t"] ["math" "x+3=6t,\\,\\,y+5=3{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+5=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=-6t"] ["math" "x+5=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=6t"] ["math" "x+5=6{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=3t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106460" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+5=3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=6t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/0e7558b9858668e619e3f7750efd76043981e2b833bcda7c7e462f6ecda1aa10.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-2,3),$ and passes through the point $(6,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-2,3),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-3=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,11),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 8=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =2pt \\\\ 4 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =p{(\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ p & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+2=(2){t}^{2},\\,y-3=2(2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+2=2{t}^{2},\\,y-3=4t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-106459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-3=-4t"] ["math" "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-3=4t"] ["math" "x+2=4{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-3=2t"] ["math" "x-2=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=4t"] ["math" "x-3=4t,\\,\\,y+2=2{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+2=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-3=4t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y+1)^{2}=-(x-3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,-1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p(x-3) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 4p & =-1 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x-1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+3=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x-3=-4{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-8t"] ["math" "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106465" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/d7a5a3f962080fe457e5d35a581ee1340f1603f0c3bc64be4de6e7553f4402e1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(2,-5),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-7).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(2,-5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y+5=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-10,7),$ we can substitute $x=-10$ and $y=7$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(-3){t}^{2},\\,y+5=2(-3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=-3{t}^{2},\\,y+5=-6t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106463" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-106463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=-6t"] ["math" "x-2=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=-6t"] ["math" "x-2=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=-2t"] ["math" "x-5=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+2=-6t"] ["math" "x-5=-6t,\\,\\,y+2=-3{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106463" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=-6t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-2)^{2}=16(x+6)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-6,2).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx+6=p{t}^{2},\\,y-2=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p(x+6) & =16(x+6) \\\\ 4p & =16 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+6=(4){t}^{2},\\,y-2=2(4)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-84847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2={t}^{2},\\,y-6=2t"] ["math" "x+6=2{t}^{2},\\,y-2=4t"] ["math" "x+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t"] ["math" "x-2=4{t}^{2},\\,y+6=2t"] ["math" "x-6=8{t}^{2},\\,y+2=4t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84847" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+6=4{t}^{2},\\,y-2=8t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx-4=-\\frac{2}{5}\\,t,\\,y+1=-\\frac{4}{25}\\,{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4),\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{5}{2}(x-4))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{4}\\,(x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{25}{4}(y+1) \\\\ {(x-4)}^{2} & =-(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-323153" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323153" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323153" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323153" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-y-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+2=-\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y+3=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-2(x+2),\\,{t}^{2}=-4(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-2(x+2))}^{2} \\\\  & =4{(x+2)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-4(y+3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+2)}^{2} & =-4(y+3) \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106503" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-106503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+2)^{2}=(y+3)"] ["math" "(x+2)^{2}=-(y+3)"] ["math" "(x+2)^{2}=-4(y+3)"] ["math" "(x+2)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}(y+3)"] ["math" "(x+3)^{2}=-(y+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-106503" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+2)^{2}=-(y+3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+5=-2t,\\,y+2=-{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+5}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=-(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+5}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(y+2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{4} & =-(y+2) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =-4(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+5)^{2}=-2(y+2)"] ["math" "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)"] ["math" "(x+5)^{2}=-8(y+2)"] ["math" "(x-5)^{2}=4(y-2)"] ["math" "(x-5)^{2}=8(y-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-84854" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+5)^{2}=-4(y+2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx-3=-{t}^{2},\\,y+4=-2t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-(x-3),\\,t=-\\frac{y+4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+4}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-(x-3)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{4} & =-(x-3) \\\\ (y+4)^{2} & =-4(x-3) \\\\ {(y+4)}^{2} & =-4(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323229" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4x+12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y-3=5t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =x,\\,t=\\frac{y-3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{y-3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=x$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{25} & =x \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =25x \\\\ {(y-3)}^{2} & =25x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323216" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c97aeff2f7dca60b3fa1b03dbaf321f1ca25e7c07f9bc8e35c41539cc4cec1f7"} {"question" "q-323216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323216" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323216" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "q-323216" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/state.json" "file_sha256" "44743fcacc020ecda83bb4e370fb3b4b1eafedc9684c246ef64ddbc80a9bca68"} {"question" "e-7446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/1a75a5242f3636efac8def7fc62458e1c1c6972ca79a1ab6fd73af47be622a7a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7446.json" "file_sha256" "b8167f286cf4ecf1acfed3d9a924e89cbccf680ebb242ba3e464dc67c0762aed"} {"question" "e-7446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5),$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-4,5),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2pt \\\\ -2=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2 ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7446.json" "file_sha256" "b8167f286cf4ecf1acfed3d9a924e89cbccf680ebb242ba3e464dc67c0762aed"} {"question" "e-7447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty),$ generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/assets/8355891fbc605593a32dd100ee8c1c2e4324da0630755984158ec23f6d419ab9.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7447.json" "file_sha256" "6289f406bf4c6acdde124e8a328d4f1d6df33a57d646fba820c07a23087aacaa"} {"question" "e-7447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2),$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k) = (-3,2),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t ,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7447.json" "file_sha256" "6289f406bf4c6acdde124e8a328d4f1d6df33a57d646fba820c07a23087aacaa"} {"question" "e-7448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7448.json" "file_sha256" "fcb937886143d1b1873c731e0916e6a2a352bf9aa846c1b9a6583e0020edb926"} {"question" "e-7448" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2},\\,t=-\\frac{y+1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{y+1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation $t^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16\\cdot (-\\frac{x-1}{2}) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-8(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007650/example-7448.json" "file_sha256" "fcb937886143d1b1873c731e0916e6a2a352bf9aa846c1b9a6583e0020edb926"}] "usage" {"e-7448" [] "e-7447" [] "e-7446" []} "basis" 1128 "retractions" [[17592186157040 :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$, generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2837/Source/Images/b479d2e8e92d2b8a69192633d94f8399.png)"] [17592186157040 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(-4,5)$, and passes through the point $(-2,3)$.\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = 2pt, \\qquad y - k = pt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (-4,5)\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2pt, \\qquad y - 5 = pt^2\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2, 3)$, we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{2 = 2pt; - 2 = pt^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 2pt \\\\\n1 &= pt \\\\\nt &= \\frac{1}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2 &= p(\\frac{1}{p})^{2} \\\\\n- 2 &= \\frac{1}{p} \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx + 4 = 2\\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t, \\qquad y - 5 = \\left(-\\dfrac12\\right)t^2\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx + 4 = -t, \\qquad y - 5 = -\\dfrac12t^2\n$$"] [17592186157041 :question/problem "What set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$, generates the parabola shown below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2837/Source/Images/90c50a502dfbd7ddf0ed06b806d52370.png)"] [17592186157041 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,2)$, and passes through the point $(1,0)$.\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx - h = pt^2, \\qquad y - k = 2pt\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (-3,2)\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\nx+3 = pt^2, \\qquad y-2 = 2pt\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,0)$, we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{4 = pt^{2}; - 2 = 2pt\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2 &= 2pt \\\\\nt &= -\\frac{1}{p}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= p(-\\frac{1}{p})^{2} \\\\\n4 &= p \\cdot \\frac{1}{p^{2}} \\\\\n4 &= \\frac{1}{p} \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t^2, \\qquad y -2 = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)t\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3 = \\dfrac{1}{4}t^2, \\qquad y -2= \\dfrac{1}{2}t\n$$"] [17592186157042 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x-1 = -2t^2, \\qquad y+1 = -4t, \\qquad t\\in(-\\infty, \\infty)$."] [17592186157042 :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= -\\frac{x - 1}{2}, t =-\\frac{y + 1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt^{2} &= (-\\frac{y + 1}{4})^{2} \\\\\n&= ((y + 1)^{2})/(16)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation\n\n$$\nt^2 = -\\dfrac{x-1}{2}\n$$\n\nand rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((y + 1)^{2})/(16) &= -\\frac{x - 1}{2} \\\\\n(y + 1)^{2} &= 16 \\cdot (-\\frac{x - 1}{2}) \\\\\n(y + 1)^{2} &= -8(x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
